{"qid":"Q734","names":["Guyana"],"title":"Guyana","endonym":{"native":"Co-operative Republic of Guyana","lang":"en"},"capsule":"The Co-operative Republic of Guyana, on the north coast of South America, was a British colony (British Guiana) until independence on 26 May 1966, becoming a republic in 1970. Under Forbes Burnham's long presidency (1964–1985) the state was declared a 'Co-operative Socialist Republic' in 1970 and nationalized the sugar and bauxite industries in the 1970s, leading to economic decline and emigration. The discovery of massive offshore oil deposits by ExxonMobil in 2015 (Stabroek Block) transformed Guyana's economic trajectory; by the early 2020s it had one of the world's fastest-growing economies, though the windfall strained regional politics given the long-standing border dispute with Venezuela over the Essequibo region.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. International boundaries are defined by colonial treaties and arbitration awards, though the western border with Venezuela remains subject to active territorial dispute and is not internationally settled.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Guyana","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Guyana"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Guyana","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guyana"},{"label":"Britannica: Forbes Burnham","url":"https://www.britannica.com/biography/Forbes-Burnham"}],"britannica":"place/Guyana","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Guyana","rulers":[{"qid":"Q718256","name":"Forbes Burnham","start":1966,"end":1980,"image":"Forbes Burnham (1966).jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Forbes%20Burnha